{"author":"Rebar Bot","author_email":"joeoakhart+bot@navapbc.com","author_id":"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d","author_sig":"{\"payload\": \"\", \"payloadType\": \"application/vnd.rebar.authorship.v1+json\", \"signatures\": [{\"keyid\": \"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d\", \"sig\": \"\"}]}","data":{"body":"## PREVENTION RECOMMENDATION (systemic guardrail against regressing the whole class)\n\nThe root cause is a **blanket base-class arm** (`isinstance(exc, LLMError) -> \"llm_unavailable\"`) that silently absorbs every NEW subclass added under `LLMError` (and, symmetrically, any new `err.error_code` string that drifts from `KNOWN_ERROR_CODES`). A new subclass ships misclassified with zero signal. Recommend TWO complementary guardrails, both **portable (in-process pytest, no CI-provider dependency)** so they satisfy `project.portability`:\n\n**1. Exhaustive exception→code contract test (the load-bearing one).**\nA `tests/unit/test_error_code_contract.py` that, via `LLMError.__subclasses__()` (recursed) — i.e. the FULL live subclass set — asserts every concrete `LLMError` subclass maps under `error_code_for()` to an **explicitly enumerated** intended code, from a table checked into the test. A subclass NOT in the table fails the test with \"new LLMError subclass X has no declared error code — add it to the contract (do not let it ride the `llm_unavailable` fallthrough).\" This converts \"silently rides the catch-all\" into a hard, local failure at the moment the subclass is introduced. Extend the same pattern to `_structured_llm_failure` (Surface 2) and the `coverage.llm_unavailable` producers (Surface 3) so all three sites are pinned to the same table — that is what makes it a CLASS fix, not a per-instance one.\n\n**2. Vocabulary-closure test.**\nAssert (a) every string literal assigned to `.error_code` in `src/rebar` (AST-scan or a small registry) is a member of `KNOWN_ERROR_CODES` — this catches the `pinned_ticket_read_failed` gap (0aee) and any future drift; and (b) every code in `KNOWN_ERROR_CODES` is reachable/produced somewhere (no dead vocabulary).\n\n**Why a test over a lint/plan-review criterion:** the mapping is data-driven and lives in one module, so a unit test that reflects over the live subclass tree is both cheaper and MORE complete than a reviewer heuristic — it cannot be forgotten and needs no LLM. A plan-review/code-review criterion (\"new exception subclass must declare an error code, never widen a catch-all\") is a useful ADVISORY backstop for the design intent, but the contract test is the enforcement.\n\n**Net effect:** adding `class NewThing(LLMError)` without a deliberate code decision breaks the build locally, so no future subclass can silently inherit the \"the AI provider is down\" misclassification — the exact regression path that produced 35ab/551b/73d8 and the three sites found in this audit."},"env_id":"eea68b7f-6697-488b-ac52-744e2ed8e96f","event_type":"COMMENT","timestamp":1788056759607723331,"uuid":"eaa02a64-f088-4967-8538-0196d697096b"}